  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The fire inspector is inspecting Claude Monet. The kitchen is in chaos. Let us see what happens next in this scene. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, может помочь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Меня он оштрафовал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Тебя депортировать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Думаю, что это?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Думаю, нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "С другим может...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Договориться удастся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хотя...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Если ты ему жизнь спасёшь...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Может, он нас и простит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Стульчик, да?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's review the scene line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, может помочь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, can I help?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'to help' is enough to make it a question. And Chef jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't know.' Notice the word order: 'know' comes first, and 'know' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Меня он оштрафовал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He fined me.' Here 'me' sets up the idea, while 'to fine' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Тебя депортировать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You must be deported.' You'll hear 'you' all the time in the show, here connected with 'deport'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Думаю, что это?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I'm thinking, what is this?' The key word here means 'I think', which Chef combines with 'what'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Думаю, нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't think so.' Notice the word order: 'I think' comes first, and 'I think' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "С другим может...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe with another...' Listen for the word meaning 'can' right alongside 'can' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Договориться удастся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We could negotiate.' Here 'to agree' sets up the idea, while 'to succeed' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There.' A compact expression built around 'there' and 'there'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хотя...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Though...' In Russian, 'at least' leads into 'at least' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Если ты ему жизнь спасёшь...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'If you save his life...' Listen for the word meaning 'you' right alongside 'him' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Может, он нас и простит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe he will forgive us.' Anya puts 'can' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'he'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Стульчик, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A chair, right?' Anya starts with 'little chair' and pairs it with 'little chair' to make the point clear. Let's listen to the dialog one more time.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
